phic to projective superspace. Figure 4(B) depicts corresponding decorated fan for n = 1."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"̸","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":146.60389743457085,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":84.84700000000005,"r":84.84700000000005,"t":156.88141624843752},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"̸"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In general, when T = Q (1) n , there are finitely many possible decorations for each ray. Namely, for ρ = R + ( a 1 x 1 + ... + a n x n ), there are 2 d -1 many possible 'square root subspaces' C ( √ a 1 θ 1 ± ... ± √ a n θ n ), where d is the number of indices i = 1 , ..., n for which a i = 0.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":145.40032273172278,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000006,"r":523.2813379200007,"t":200.3405929484377},"charspan":[0,295],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"In general, when T = Q (1) n , there are finitely many possible decorations for each ray. Namely, for ρ = R + ( a 1 x 1 + ... + a n x n ), there are 2 d -1 many possible 'square root subspaces' C ( √ a 1 θ 1 ± ... ± √ a n θ n ), where d is the number of indices i = 1 , ..., n for which a i = 0."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The prior example admitted no issues of compatibility between different rays of the same cone. This is not ordinarily the case; if ρ 1 = R + ( a 1 x 1 + ... + a n x n ) and ρ 2 = R + ( b 1 x 1 + ... + b n x n ), compatibility is less common if the linear matroid on the n vectors","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":103.55732273172282,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.00000000000006,"r":523.2789075200002,"t":142.93341624843754},"charspan":[0,279],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"The prior example admitted no issues of compatibility between different rays of the same cone. This is not ordinarily the case; if ρ 1 = R + ( a 1 x 1 + ... + a n x